Draw the **ice floe**, a 32x32 platform sprite for an arctic crossing game, using
only the drawing tool.

The ice floe is the safe platform: a flat pan of drifting ice the player hops onto
to cross the open water. It must read as calm, flat, solid footing — a safe place
to stand — and be clearly distinct from the jagged, dangerous ice hazard. Draw it
smooth and rounded, filling most of the frame, on full transparency.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw` binary already on your `PATH`. It is the
sole channel: you build the sprite by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the pixels on disk. An image produced
any other way (writing a PNG directly, editing `canvas.png` by hand) is discarded
and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

After each call the binary re-renders the current image to `canvas.png` and
appends the operation to `actions.json`. A blank starting canvas and an empty log
are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
draw fill-rect --x 3 --y 6 --width 26 --height 22 --color "#c3dee9"
```

Run `draw --help` to list every operation and `draw <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`canvas.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the floe sprite up operation by operation until the image in `canvas.png`
matches the brief — a flat, smooth, rounded ice pan filling most of the frame, with
a snow-dusted top surface, a ringed ice edge, and a waterline shadow so it reads as
floating, calm and safe (not a jagged hazard), in the pale ice palette on full
transparency. When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded `actions.json` is your
finished submission.
